-----------------------\nAsymptotic growth and least common multiples in groups (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=8cd1ab2d4f0ec46eded55fe6f9f304e13e92436c&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Asymptotic growth and least common multiples in groups ... Bou-Rabee∗and D. ... McReynolds† ... keywords: free groups, hyperbolic groups, residual ﬁniteness, normal subgroup growth, word growth.\nAsymptotic growth and least common multiples in groups\n1\nAsymptotic growth and\nleast common multiples in groups\nK. Bou-Rabee∗and D. B. McReynolds†\nApril 9, 2011\nAbstract\nIn this article we relate word and normal subgroup growth to certain functions that arise in the\nquantiﬁcation of residual ﬁniteness. One consequence of this endeavor is a pair of results that\nequate the virtual nilpotency of a ﬁnitely generated group with the asymptotic behavior of these\nfunctions. The second half of this article investigates the asymptotic behavior of two of these\nfunctions. Our main result in this area resolves a question of Bogopolski from the Kourovka\nnotebook concerning lower bounds of one of these functions for nonabelian free groups.\n1991 MSC classes: 20F32, 20E26\nkeywords: free groups, hyperbolic groups, residual ﬁniteness, normal subgroup growth, word growth.\n1\nIntroduction\nThe goals of the present article are to examine the interplay between word and normal subgroup\ngrowth, and to quantify residual ﬁniteness, a topic motivated and described by the ﬁrst author in [1].\nThese two goals have an intimate relationship that will be illustrated throughout this article.\nOur focus begins with the interplay between word and normal subgroup growth. Recall that for a\nﬁxed ﬁnite generating set X of Γ with associated word metric ||·||X, word growth investigates the\nasymptotic behavior of the function\nwΓ,X(n) = |{γ ∈Γ : ||γ||X ≤n}|,\nwhile normal subgroup growth investigates the asymptotic behavior of the function\ns◁\nΓ (n) = |{∆◁Γ : [Γ : ∆] ≤n}|.\nTo study the interaction between word and subgroup growth we propose the ﬁrst of a pair of ques-\ntions:\n∗University of Chicago, Chicago, IL 60637. E-mail: khalid@math.uchicago.edu\n†University of Chicago, Chicago, IL 60637. E-mail: dmcreyn@math.uchicago.edu--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResidual Finiteness Growths of Virtually Special Groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.6974)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.6 years ago; Authors: Khalid Bou-Rabee, Mark F. ... Indeed, the special linear group $\\text{SL}_k(\\mathbb{Z})$, for $k > 2$, has residual finiteness growth $n^{k-1}$.\nTitle: Residual Finiteness Growths of Virtually Special Groups\nAuthors: Khalid Bou-Rabee, Mark F. Hagen, Priyam Patel\nDate: Thu Feb 27 17:04:02 2014\n\nLet $G$ be a virtually special group. Then the residual finiteness growth of $G$ is at most linear. This result cannot be found by embedding $G$ into a special linear group. Indeed, the special linear group $\\text{SL}_k(\\mathbb{Z})$, for $k > 2$, has residual finiteness growth $n^{k-1}$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBertrand's postulate and subgroup growth - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021869310001791)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.2 years ago; Crawled: last week; Bou-Rabee, D.B.McReynolds ... Using these functions, their connection to residual finiteness, and integration theory on profinite groups, we define the residual average of a finitely generated group.\n\n## Journal of Algebra\n\nDate: 15 August 2010\n\nPages: 793-819\n\nVolume: Volume 324, Issue 4\n\nView accessibility information\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nOpen archive\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# Bertrand's postulate and subgroup growth\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel K. Bou-Rabee, D.B. McReynolds\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.jalgebra.2010.03.031\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this article we investigate the {L}^{1}-norm of certain functions on groups called divisibility functions. Using these functions, their connection to residual finiteness, and integration theory on profinite groups, we define the residual average of a finitely generated group. One of the main results in this article is the finiteness of residual averages on finitely generated linear groups. Whether or not the residual average is finite depends on growth rates of indices of finite index subgroups. Our results on index growth rates are analogous to results on gaps between primes, and provide a variant of the subgroup growth function, which may be of independent interest.\n\n## MSC\n\n20E07\n\n;\n\n20E18\n\n## Keywords\n\nBertrand's postulate\n\n;\n\nResidual finiteness\n\n;\n\nSubgroup growth--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the spectrum of residual finiteness growth functions - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022404925002385)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; In [4] Bou-Rabee and Seward constructed examples of finitely generated residually finite groups G whose residual finiteness growth function {\\mathcal{F}}_{G} can be at least as fast as any prescribed function. ... Besides {\\mathcal{F}}_{G}^{S}, we study the full residual finiteness growth function (also called residual girth) {\\mathcal{R}}_{G}^{S} introduced by Bou-Rabee and McReynolds [3] and the divisibility function {\\mathcal{D}}_{G}^{S} (also called the non-normal residual finiteness growth function).\n\n## Journal of Pure and Applied Algebra\n\nVolume 229, Issue 11, November 2025, 108099\n\n# On the spectrum of residual finiteness growth functions\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpaa.2025.108099Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn [4] Bou-Rabee and Seward constructed examples of finitely generated residually finite groups G whose residual finiteness growth function {\\mathcal{F}}_{G} can be at least as fast as any prescribed function. In this note we describe a modified version of their construction, which allows us to give a complementary upper bound on {\\mathcal{F}}_{G}. As such, every nondecreasing function at least is close to the residual finiteness growth function of some finitely generated group. We also have similar result for the full residual finiteness growth function and for the divisibility function.\n\n## Introduction\n\nA group G is residually finite if every nontrivial element can be distinguished from the identity in some finite quotient of G. In [1] Bou-Rabee pioneered the study of an effective version of residual finiteness, valid when G is generated by a finite set S, and measured by the residual finiteness growth function of G. The project of estimating {\\mathcal{F}}_{G}^{S} for groups G of particular interest has been taken up by many authors; see [2] Table 1 for a summary of some of the known estimates. One may also pose the “inverse” problem and ask which functions can arise as the residual finiteness growth of some group G. For instance, one might ask whether there is a universal upper bound on the rate at which {\\mathcal{F}}_{G}^{S} can grow. Bou-Rabee and Seward [4] answer this last question in the negative: they construct, for any increasing function F, a finitely generated residually finite group G such that {\\mathcal{F}}_{G}^{S} dominates F. Our goal is to strengthen their result, by showing that for all sufficiently quickly growing F, we can arrange that {\\mathcal{F}}_{G}^{S} is equivalent to F, in an appropriate sense.\n\nTheorem 1.1 Theorem 3.12\n\nLet be a nondecreasing function such that:\n\n  * (a)\n\nThere exist such that for all ,\n\n  * (b)\n\nThere exist such that for all ,\n\nThen there exists a residually finite group G, generated be a finite set S, and such that for all ,\n\nIn the terminology of [8], inequalities of the form (1) constitute a strong equivalence between the functions F and {\\mathcal{F}}_{G}^{S}. Absent a condition of the form (b) on F, we can still show an equivalence between the growth of {\\mathcal{F}}_{G} and F, in a slightly weaker sense (see Theorem 3.9 below for the general statement).\n\nThere are many equivalent ways to define residual finiteness of a group, some of which lead to distinct growth functions. Besides {\\mathcal{F}}_{G}^{S}, we study the full residual finiteness growth function (also called residual girth) {\\mathcal{R}}_{G}^{S} introduced by Bou-Rabee and McReynolds [3] and the divisibility function {\\mathcal{D}}_{G}^{S} (also called the non-normal residual finiteness growth function). For any finitely generated group G, we have . --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAsymptotic growth and least common multiples in groups | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/0907.3681)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.7 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Bou-Rabee ... McReynolds ... In this article we relate word and subgroup growth to certain functions that arise in the quantification of residual finiteness. ... @misc{bourabee2010asymptoticgrowthcommonmultiples, title={Asymptotic growth and least common multiples in groups}, author={K.\nSubmitted 27 Jan 2010\n\n# Asymptotic growth and least common multiples in groups\n\nKB\n\nK. Bou-Rabee\n\nDB\n\nD. B. McReynolds\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this article we relate word and subgroup growth to certain functions that arise in the quantification of residual finiteness. One consequence of this endeavor is a pair of results that equate the nilpotency of a finitely generated group with the asymptotic behavior of these functions. The second half of this article investigates the asymptotic behavior of two of these functions. Our main result in this arena resolves a question of Bogopolski from the Kourovka notebook concerning lo